1
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:06,400
Congratulations!

2
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:11,600
You're now in charge of your retail
company's newly developed customer service
agent.

3
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,161
We're signed in as Alex,

4
00:00:13,161 --> 00:00:17,520
a customer who wants to exchange a pair of
shoes for a larger size.

5
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:22,080
Notice the customer vaguely described what
they want, as customers do.

6
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:24,616
And now the agent needs to work out how to

7
00:00:24,616 --> 00:00:28,480
respond using its instructions and the
capabilities that it has.

8
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:33,120
Of course the agent needs the order number,
so let's go ahead and supply that.

9
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:35,520
And now it's able to look up the shoes.

10
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:39,196
Now given the order number, the agent
successfully found the order,

11
00:00:39,196 --> 00:00:41,994
which is a pair of blue sneakers in a size 8
and it

12
00:00:41,994 --> 00:00:46,000
then asks the customer which size that
they'd like to replace these with.

13
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,505
So let's go ahead and say a 9 and then the
agent

14
00:00:49,505 --> 00:00:55,200
comes back and says that it can't actually
check availability or do exchanges.

15
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:59,120
So why did it ask us that question in the
first place?

16
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:01,811
This is the kind of stuff that annoys
customers

17
00:01:01,811 --> 00:01:04,560
when your company rolls out its fancy new
agent.

18
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:06,640
As engineers, we can make this better.

19
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:10,400
First, we need to understand why the agent
responded this way.

20
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:12,686
So let's open up its definition and look

21
00:01:12,686 --> 00:01:16,000
at the instructions and capabilities that
it's been given.

22
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,720
The agent is built using the open source
framework Mastra and here's its definition.

23
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:23,040
It needs a model to process the information.

24
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:25,120
It's given and generate a response.

25
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:28,000
And this is your first design decision to
make.

26
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,440
So you shouldn't reach for the biggest, most
expensive model on the market by default.

27
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:37,760
Remember you have end users that are
engaging with this agent.

28
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:41,437
So ask yourself, given the task that your
agent needs to do,

29
00:01:41,437 --> 00:01:45,360
what's the most efficient yet affordable
model that you can use.

30
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:47,381
So we're using GPT-6 Luna here,

31
00:01:47,381 --> 00:01:52,400
which is a smaller model because the agent's
tasks are fairly focused, right?

32
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:55,094
It just needs to identify some missing
information,

33
00:01:55,094 --> 00:01:58,000
request the order lookup and respond using
the results.

34
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,640
So smaller models generally cost less and
they can respond faster.

35
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:06,320
So they're a useful starting point for this
kind of task.

36
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:11,612
And you can test whether the model follows
instructions and it uses its tools

37
00:02:11,612 --> 00:02:16,560
correctly and if it does struggle then you
can try a more capable model.

38
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:19,120
Now let's look at the instructions.

39
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:24,238
Now the instructions are critical system
level prompts that define the agent's
identity

40
00:02:24,238 --> 00:02:29,120
and expertise and we can also use these
instructions to guide the agent's behavior.

41
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:33,360
For example, we state that the agent is not
allowed to make up details.

42
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:38,153
Instead, we tell it what to do if the tool
isn't available and we also give

43
00:02:38,153 --> 00:02:43,840
it permission to fail versus making up order
details just to give the customer an answer.

44
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:47,600
Also we give it permission to ask for
missing information.

45
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:51,120
That's why it asks Alex for an order number.

46
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:53,167
The lookup needs that information,

47
00:02:53,167 --> 00:02:56,720
so we've told the agent to ask for it rather
than to guess.

48
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:59,680
And then we can also give the agents tools.

49
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:03,820
So this is what allows the agent to actually
take actions and these

50
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:08,640
can be tools that we've defined and
implemented or they can be external tools.

51
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:12,996
For example, here is where we've registered
the get order tool with the

52
00:03:12,996 --> 00:03:16,800
agent and this tool maps to a function that
we've implemented.

53
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:21,920
Now registering the tool makes it available
to the model along with its description.

54
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:26,400
And that description explains what the tool
does and when to use it.

55
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:31,120
Now notice that get orders availability is
conditional on the order lookup setting.

56
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:33,734
So you could use this same approach to make
a tool

57
00:03:33,734 --> 00:03:37,760
available based on anything like the
customer's permissions or anything else.

58
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:42,640
And then there's 2 other tools here that
we've created as custom memory helpers.

59
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:45,040
In fact, let's look at memory.

60
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:48,720
So here we're defining how our agent's
memory should work.

61
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:53,120
So we're supplying earlier messages so that
the agent can follow the conversation.

62
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:55,858
When Alex replies with just an order number,

63
00:03:55,858 --> 00:03:59,840
those earlier messages explain what Alex was
even talking about.

64
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:04,719
Here we include the latest 20 messages from
this thread and we could send

65
00:04:04,719 --> 00:04:07,860
the entire conversation but the model
processes

66
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:10,734
that history with every request right so as

67
00:04:10,734 --> 00:04:16,682
the conversation grows that means more
tokens to pay for and potentially slower
responses

68
00:04:16,682 --> 00:04:21,760
models also have a limit on how much
information they can receive in 1 call.

69
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:27,360
So keeping the last 20 messages puts a bound
on how much history that we're sending.

70
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:29,360
But there is a trade-off, right?

71
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:33,760
An important detail from earlier in the
conversation might get left out.

72
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:36,400
And 20 isn't a magic number.

73
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:40,365
We needed to choose a limit that gives our
agent enough history

74
00:04:40,365 --> 00:04:44,960
to handle the customer's request without
sending more than what it needs.

75
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:48,475
Now 1 fascinating thing about memory is that
there are several

76
00:04:48,475 --> 00:04:52,160
different types of memory and all of them
work a bit differently.

77
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:57,423
For example, semantic recall would retrieve
relevant messages based

78
00:04:57,423 --> 00:05:00,880
on their meaning rather than exact keywords.

79
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:06,480
We've turned that off so that we're using
recent messages without that extra search.

80
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:11,360
And then working memory lets the agent keep
information across conversations.

81
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:14,898
So we've turned this off as well because we
don't

82
00:05:14,898 --> 00:05:20,240
want the agent automatically carrying order
details into its next request.

83
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:24,560
Now we still want customers to be able to
save shopping preferences.

84
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:28,640
And for that, we've registered read
preference and save preference tools.

85
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:32,664
So the agent can save a preference when the
customer asks it

86
00:05:32,664 --> 00:05:37,760
to remember 1 and then it can retrieve a
preference in another conversation.

87
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:41,962
So buying a pair of shoes or requesting a
replacement

88
00:05:41,962 --> 00:05:46,560
size doesn't by itself ask the agent to save
a preference.

89
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:50,130
Now all of these configurations together
shape how the

90
00:05:50,130 --> 00:05:55,683
agent responds and it also gives us a hint
of which parts of the agent we might need

91
00:05:55,683 --> 00:05:59,120
to observe and tweak when his behavior is
not ideal.

92
00:05:59,120 --> 00:06:02,473
Now remember we saw the agent asked Alex for
more information,

93
00:06:02,473 --> 00:06:04,960
even though it couldn't complete the
exchange.

94
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:08,320
The instructions that we've given it shape
that behavior.

95
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:11,110
If we're telling it to collect replacement
details,

96
00:06:11,110 --> 00:06:14,228
then we need to consider whether collecting
those details

97
00:06:14,228 --> 00:06:16,800
is useful with the tools that it currently
has.

98
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:21,360
Or maybe we can even extend its capabilities
by providing more tools.

99
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:24,480
So that's why we're looking at how this
agent is configured.

100
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:27,531
We want to understand what's leading it to
ask those

101
00:06:27,531 --> 00:06:30,640
questions so that we can decide what needs
to change.

102
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:34,800
In this case, we can already see why it's
asking for replacement details.

103
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:38,800
We've instructed it to do that, even though
it can't complete the exchange.

104
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:41,061
But when you're working on an agent,

105
00:06:41,061 --> 00:06:46,462
it might not always be that obvious right um
you might get an incorrect answer and you

106
00:06:46,462 --> 00:06:49,100
need to find out whether the tool returned

107
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:52,617
the wrong information or the model used that
information

108
00:06:52,617 --> 00:06:55,506
incorrectly so let's use this request to
learn

109
00:06:55,506 --> 00:06:58,332
how to inspect what happened during a run
and

110
00:06:58,332 --> 00:07:03,920
we'll follow the order lookup and see how
the result became a part of the agent's
answer.

111
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:06,617
So many people treat agents as black boxes,

112
00:07:06,617 --> 00:07:11,760
but as engineers we need to know how to peek
inside that box and what to look for.

113
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:14,640
So let's check the trace in Mastra Studio.

114
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:18,400
A trace is a record of what the agent did
during 1 run.

115
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:20,991
We can use this to check whether the agent
called the

116
00:07:20,991 --> 00:07:24,560
right tool and whether that tool returned
the information that it needed.

117
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:28,080
So here's the run from when Alex supplied
the order number.

118
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:34,400
In the trace details, we can follow the
steps that the agent took to answer Alex.

119
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:37,280
So this tree shows what happened during the
run.

120
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:42,224
The conversation history is loaded from
memory, then the model works through its
steps,

121
00:07:42,224 --> 00:07:45,520
calling tools as needed, and then finally
the conversation

122
00:07:46,400 --> 00:07:48,960
is saved for the next message.

123
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:51,040
The numbering starts at 0.

124
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:54,640
So step 0 is the first step.

125
00:07:54,640 --> 00:08:00,360
Now under input we have our instructions
from our agent definition that's going

126
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:04,560
to be in every single step which is why I
said whatever is

127
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:10,135
in those instructions is critical and then
the input also includes the recent

128
00:08:10,135 --> 00:08:16,000
conversation history up to 20 messages which
we configured in the memory setting.

129
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,360
Okay now let's take a look at the output.

130
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:23,415
So the model has made a tool request and it
wants the agent to

131
00:08:23,415 --> 00:08:28,320
run the get order tool and it supplied
Alex's order number as the argument.

132
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:31,157
The agent then runs that function which we
can

133
00:08:31,157 --> 00:08:34,240
inspect in a separate get order entry in the
tree.

134
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:39,120
So input shows what the function received
and output shows what it returned.

135
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:44,880
So here the lookup found Alex's order and
returned the blue sneaker in size 8.

136
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:48,560
Now the database access happens in our code.

137
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:51,814
We don't need the model to figure out how to
query

138
00:08:51,814 --> 00:08:56,240
the database or decide whether Alex is
allowed to access this order.

139
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:01,360
We've already defined those rules so we can
enforce them with deterministic code.

140
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:05,722
And that gives us a predictable check
without paying

141
00:09:05,722 --> 00:09:10,000
for another model call or waiting for its
response.

142
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:11,840
And then it also helps with debugging.

143
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:17,668
So if the agent gave Alex the wrong shoe
size, then this is 1 place that we would
look if

144
00:09:17,668 --> 00:09:21,138
the value was wrong in the tool result we
checked the

145
00:09:21,138 --> 00:09:24,281
lookup code or the stored order right but if
the

146
00:09:24,281 --> 00:09:28,210
value was correct here and then wrong in the
agent's answer,

147
00:09:28,210 --> 00:09:31,680
then we'd investigate how the agent used
that result.

148
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:33,120
Let's look at the next step.

149
00:09:33,120 --> 00:09:37,840
So along with the conversation, there is
also now a result

150
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:41,680
from the call to get order.

151
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:45,674
So the agent sent that result back to the
model for another call,

152
00:09:45,674 --> 00:09:49,360
giving it information that it didn't have in
the first step.

153
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:54,385
And this cycle where the agent repeatedly
presents the model with information and

154
00:09:54,385 --> 00:09:58,480
then the model responds to the agent with
the next action to take.

155
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:01,200
This is what's known as the agentic loop.

156
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:06,000
So when the model requests a tool, the agent
runs it and sends the result back.

157
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:11,440
And then the model can then use that result
to respond or request another tool.

158
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:15,600
And note that finishing a run doesn't mean
that the customer's problem is solved.

159
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:18,880
In Alex's case, the model responded with a
question, right?

160
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:22,658
So this run ended and the agent waited for
another customer

161
00:10:22,658 --> 00:10:27,332
message as you're developing your agents 1
important thing to consider is

162
00:10:27,332 --> 00:10:30,726
how many times the model can continue this
loop so if

163
00:10:30,726 --> 00:10:34,632
a tool keeps returning an error for example
we don't want the

164
00:10:34,632 --> 00:10:38,794
agent to keep trying indefinitely while the
customer waits and we

165
00:10:38,794 --> 00:10:41,803
pay for more calls so we can set a limit on
how

166
00:10:41,803 --> 00:10:45,133
many model steps a run can take so here in
our agent

167
00:10:45,133 --> 00:10:49,295
definition we've added a default option that
sets max steps to 5,

168
00:10:49,295 --> 00:10:52,240
which gives each run a limit of 5 model
steps.

169
00:10:52,240 --> 00:10:57,360
A step is a call to the model and that call
can request more than 1 tool.

170
00:10:57,360 --> 00:11:00,400
So 5 steps doesn't mean 5 tool calls.

171
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:04,960
It means the model can be called up to 5
times during that run.

172
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:07,280
It's also a maximum, not a target, right?

173
00:11:07,280 --> 00:11:12,240
So the agent can respond before it reaches
that limit, uh, which would be great.

174
00:11:12,240 --> 00:11:15,440
So how do you choose a suitable limit for
your agent?

175
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:18,960
Well, you'll need to test the work that the
agent needs to do.

176
00:11:18,960 --> 00:11:21,816
So you can run multiple scenarios via evals,

177
00:11:21,816 --> 00:11:25,840
which we'll cover in detail in a later
chapter in this course.

178
00:11:25,840 --> 00:11:29,036
And we could measure how many steps it takes
an

179
00:11:29,036 --> 00:11:34,000
agent to get to a successful result without
excessive floundering, right?

180
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,400
So our agent needed 2 steps.

181
00:11:36,400 --> 00:11:39,128
So 5 gives it a bit of buffer now back to

182
00:11:39,128 --> 00:11:43,853
the agent's annoying behavior where it's
quizzing the user for exchange

183
00:11:43,853 --> 00:11:48,112
details even though it has no capability to
process exchanges we

184
00:11:48,112 --> 00:11:51,440
now know where to go to fix this so let's do
that.

185
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:55,403
Here in the instructions is where we're
telling the agent to collect

186
00:11:55,403 --> 00:11:59,600
replacement details and we also tell it that
it can't process exchanges.

187
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:03,520
So we've given it instructions to gather
information that it can't use.

188
00:12:03,520 --> 00:12:07,760
So really this is our fault and then people
blame it on AI.

189
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:10,720
As engineers we can change that behavior.

190
00:12:10,720 --> 00:12:15,069
For now, we wanted to explain that exchanges
aren't

191
00:12:15,069 --> 00:12:19,760
supported and to stop asking exchange
questions, right?

192
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:24,080
So it should still help when a customer asks
to look up an order.

193
00:12:24,080 --> 00:12:27,360
So let's go ahead and change that and we'll
leave everything else the same.

194
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:30,160
The model, the order, and the tools.

195
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:34,000
I'm starting a new thread so that the agent
has fresh context.

196
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,280
Let's go ahead and ask about the exchange
again.

197
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:39,120
Great, that's much better.

198
00:12:39,120 --> 00:12:42,640
Models are non-deterministic, but we do have
a bit of control.

199
00:12:42,640 --> 00:12:46,240
So the system instructions are included with
every call to the model.

200
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:49,680
That means the agent gets that guidance
again on every turn.

201
00:12:49,680 --> 00:12:51,760
Now I don't want to mislead you.

202
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:55,815
Instructions don't guarantee that the model
will always behave as intended,

203
00:12:55,815 --> 00:12:59,600
but at least you've done your part to guide
it in the right direction.

204
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:01,840
The model itself is stateless.

205
00:13:01,840 --> 00:13:06,824
It has no recollection of previous calls, so
every time our agent calls the model,

206
00:13:06,824 --> 00:13:11,200
it has to supply all of the information that
the model needs to respond.

207
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:14,400
And that information is its context.

208
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:19,440
So the context window is the limit on how
much the model can work with at once.

209
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:23,373
The system instructions are part of that
context and our agent

210
00:13:23,373 --> 00:13:27,370
also includes up to the last 20 messages
from the conversation,

211
00:13:27,370 --> 00:13:30,859
which is inclusive of the user's prompts as
well as the

212
00:13:30,859 --> 00:13:35,744
model's responses and it also supplies
information about the available tools,

213
00:13:35,744 --> 00:13:41,200
such as their names and descriptions, along
with the arguments that each tool accepts.

214
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:47,040
So if a tool has already run, then its
result is included there as well.

215
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:52,960
As the conversation grows, we need to decide
what to keep sending, right?

216
00:13:52,960 --> 00:13:59,034
Remember that we've limited the message
history to 20 messages and some of the

217
00:13:59,034 --> 00:14:04,640
older messages before then may still be
relevant and some of it may not.

218
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:07,360
So compaction is helpful here.

219
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:10,262
It's where older messages are compressed
into

220
00:14:10,262 --> 00:14:13,680
shorter notes that are included with recent
messages.

221
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:17,760
The trade-off is that those notes may leave
out some details.

222
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:22,320
We can also save specific information
separately from the conversation.

223
00:14:22,320 --> 00:14:24,585
Our agent can retrieve a preference,

224
00:14:24,585 --> 00:14:28,800
for example when it's needed and include it
in the model's context.

225
00:14:28,800 --> 00:14:30,320
Let's see how that works.

226
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:34,960
So let's first ask whether the agent has a
save shoe size for Alex.

227
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:37,626
So we see that it calls the read preference
tool,

228
00:14:37,626 --> 00:14:41,760
which is a tool that we implemented to read
this information from the store.

229
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:43,948
The result says that it wasn't found,

230
00:14:43,948 --> 00:14:48,560
which is exactly what we expect because we
have not saved this preference yet.

231
00:14:48,560 --> 00:14:50,080
So let's go ahead and store it.

232
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:53,600
Please remember my preferred shoe size is 9.

233
00:14:53,600 --> 00:14:57,879
This time the model calls the save
preference tool,

234
00:14:57,879 --> 00:15:03,920
which writes that preference to the database
and returns a confirmation.

235
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:06,800
Okay, let's try again in a new thread.

236
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:07,840
Wonderful.

237
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:10,013
So we know that our agent is capable of

238
00:15:10,013 --> 00:15:13,301
remembering and recalling things outside of
a chat session,

239
00:15:13,301 --> 00:15:15,920
but there's a bit more to consider here,
right?

240
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:20,800
When you add memory to an agent, decide
what's worth remembering.

241
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:23,440
Let's look at the functions for these tools.

242
00:15:23,440 --> 00:15:28,880
Now we're making a deliberate choice about
what should carry across conversations.

243
00:15:29,040 --> 00:15:33,425
A customer might discuss 1 order today and a
different order tomorrow and we

244
00:15:33,425 --> 00:15:38,560
don't want a remembered order number
deciding which purchase their next request
is about.

245
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:42,476
Remember that we've disabled automatic
working memory in the agent

246
00:15:42,476 --> 00:15:47,520
definition and instead we provided those
tools specifically for shopping preferences.

247
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:52,884
The Save Tools schema accepts categories
such as shoe size and shirt size and the
tool

248
00:15:52,884 --> 00:15:55,691
description tells the model to save only
when

249
00:15:55,691 --> 00:15:58,560
the customer asks it to remember a
preference.

250
00:15:58,560 --> 00:16:03,200
And our agent pulls the customer's identity
when it reads or writes a preference.

251
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:07,282
And the database stores each category
separately for each customer

252
00:16:07,282 --> 00:16:11,920
so another customer's lookup doesn't return
Alex's preferences for example.

253
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:14,960
Let's dive a bit more into the power of
tools.

254
00:16:14,960 --> 00:16:20,000
Here in our agent definition we've seen the
agent retrieve order details using Get
Order.

255
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,749
Now I want to show you what happens if we
remove that tool

256
00:16:23,749 --> 00:16:28,080
and how the agent responds when it can no
longer look up the order.

257
00:16:28,080 --> 00:16:33,033
Now remember that get order is conditionally
available and this order lookup

258
00:16:33,033 --> 00:16:37,920
setting controls whether it's included in
the tools that we give the model.

259
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:40,080
So I'm gonna change it to false.

260
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:45,840
And now if we look in studio, we see that
get order is no longer available to the
agent.

261
00:16:45,920 --> 00:16:49,120
In a new thread, let's ask it about an
order.

262
00:16:49,120 --> 00:16:53,208
So the agent sent the model, the system
instructions, the user's prompts,

263
00:16:53,208 --> 00:16:55,840
and info about the tools that it had access
to,

264
00:16:55,840 --> 00:16:58,640
which are only the read and save preferences
tool.

265
00:16:58,640 --> 00:17:02,767
The model doesn't have access to the
database right and it only

266
00:17:02,767 --> 00:17:07,091
has access to whatever information the agent
has provided to it so

267
00:17:07,091 --> 00:17:10,760
when we remove this tool from the agent it
no longer has

268
00:17:10,760 --> 00:17:14,560
access to that tool so it can't share that
with the model.

269
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:17,920
So tools are what agents use to take
actions.

270
00:17:17,920 --> 00:17:22,400
I'm gonna revert this back to true and then
let's inspect the tool definition.

271
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:25,714
So the description says retrieve an order
belonging to the

272
00:17:25,714 --> 00:17:29,200
current customer and that tells the model
what the tool does.

273
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:33,937
The next sentence says that it should use it
to verify order details before

274
00:17:33,937 --> 00:17:38,800
answering questions about a purchase and
that tells the model when to use it.

275
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:41,312
So we've kept that guidance with the tool

276
00:17:41,312 --> 00:17:44,560
rather than repeating it in the agent's
instructions.

277
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:47,372
And the instructions still say not to invent
order

278
00:17:47,372 --> 00:17:50,240
details and what to do when lookup isn't
available.

279
00:17:50,240 --> 00:17:54,240
We need those rules even when this tool
isn't registered.

280
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:58,144
Now the input schema defines the input that
the tool accepts,

281
00:17:58,144 --> 00:18:00,320
which in this case is an order ID.

282
00:18:00,320 --> 00:18:04,560
And the execute function contains the
application code that performs the lookup.

283
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:06,400
So those parts have different jobs.

284
00:18:06,400 --> 00:18:10,000
A clear description helps the model select
the appropriate tool.

285
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:13,093
A schema lets the application validate the
shape of

286
00:18:13,093 --> 00:18:17,520
the request and then the implementation
determines what actually happens.

287
00:18:17,520 --> 00:18:20,463
Now, an order ID being a valid string
doesn't mean

288
00:18:20,463 --> 00:18:23,230
that the customer is allowed to see that
order,

289
00:18:23,230 --> 00:18:26,880
so we also need to check access in the
implementation as well.

290
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:28,880
Here's where we enforce access.

291
00:18:28,880 --> 00:18:36,320
The query requires both the requested order
ID and the current customer's ID to match.

292
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:40,056
So this is common since programming, but
let's face it, you know,

293
00:18:40,056 --> 00:18:42,240
many of us aren't coding much anymore.

294
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:47,245
So I just want to point out how important it
is for us to remember the logic,

295
00:18:47,245 --> 00:18:50,560
even if we're not the ones that's writing
the code.

296
00:18:50,560 --> 00:18:53,920
So if Alex applies someone else's order
number,

297
00:18:53,920 --> 00:18:58,424
that combination won't match and the
function returns order not

298
00:18:58,424 --> 00:19:04,000
found for this customer without returning
the other customer's orders details.

299
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:09,920
So the model can request the lookup, but our
code controls which record it can retrieve.

300
00:19:09,920 --> 00:19:14,560
And that access check runs every time the
tool performs a lookup.

301
00:19:14,560 --> 00:19:20,400
So we aren't relying on the model to follow
some instructions about privacy.

302
00:19:20,400 --> 00:19:23,370
So 1 more thing before we wrap up this
chapter,

303
00:19:23,370 --> 00:19:26,720
I want to touch on how we test this agent
with evals.

304
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:29,509
So evals are tests where we give the agent a

305
00:19:29,509 --> 00:19:33,440
request and then we check his behavior
against what we expect.

306
00:19:33,440 --> 00:19:39,280
And these cases use our customer Alex in
orders that we've created as test data.

307
00:19:39,280 --> 00:19:41,862
Because we know what's in those orders,

308
00:19:41,862 --> 00:19:46,960
we can check whether the agent retrieves and
reports the correct information.

309
00:19:46,960 --> 00:19:48,666
For a production agent,

310
00:19:48,666 --> 00:19:54,080
we expand this set to cover different
customers and different situations.

311
00:19:54,080 --> 00:19:54,468
Okay,

312
00:19:54,468 --> 00:20:01,068
each case describes the behavior that we
want rather than requiring 1 exact sentence,

313
00:20:01,068 --> 00:20:02,000
for example.

314
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,116
So for a direct order question,

315
00:20:04,116 --> 00:20:09,440
we check that the agent calls get order and
reports the correct purchase item.

316
00:20:09,440 --> 00:20:11,200
A plausible answer isn't enough.

317
00:20:11,200 --> 00:20:14,240
We want to see that it used the store's
data.

318
00:20:14,240 --> 00:20:15,562
For an exchange request,

319
00:20:15,562 --> 00:20:19,254
we check that the agent explains its
limitations without collecting

320
00:20:19,254 --> 00:20:22,560
more details or claiming that an exchange
has been approved.

321
00:20:22,560 --> 00:20:25,360
So this checks whether the instruction
change we made actually

322
00:20:26,320 --> 00:20:28,640
stops that unnecessary behavior.

323
00:20:28,640 --> 00:20:31,552
And then when querying another customer's
order,

324
00:20:31,552 --> 00:20:34,343
we check that the lookup returns no
accessible

325
00:20:34,343 --> 00:20:37,680
order and that the answer doesn't disclose
its context.

326
00:20:37,680 --> 00:20:40,550
So this checks both the access rule in our

327
00:20:40,550 --> 00:20:44,240
code and how the agent responds when access
is denied.

328
00:20:44,240 --> 00:20:46,812
So just like any application test,

329
00:20:46,812 --> 00:20:53,166
it's a good idea to rerun these cases after
changing the agent's instructions or its

330
00:20:53,166 --> 00:20:59,520
tools or its model to check whether a change
broke anything that was working before.

331
00:20:59,520 --> 00:21:04,827
In this chapter we explore the primitives
that make up an agent and how the model,

332
00:21:04,827 --> 00:21:07,092
the system instructions, the tools,

333
00:21:07,092 --> 00:21:11,040
context and memory all come together within
the agentic loop.

334
00:21:11,040 --> 00:21:16,240
As engineers, we decide how to configure
these pieces and we can inspect their

335
00:21:16,240 --> 00:21:21,440
behavior and tweak these configurations to
have the agent work as we intended.

336
00:21:21,440 --> 00:21:22,740
In the next chapter,

337
00:21:22,740 --> 00:21:28,460
we'll build on this retail agent and we'll
start giving it some capabilities to
actually

338
00:21:28,460 --> 00:21:33,920
handle an exchange and introduce a decision
model to help it decide what to do next.
